+# Click limits
+
+Short links can be limited to a number of successful redirects - useful for single-use invitations and
+one-time downloads.
+
+## Creating a limited link (FR-1)
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/links
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{ "url": "https://example.com/invite", "maxClicks": 1 }
+```
+
+`maxClicks` is optional and must be between 1 and 1,000,000; other values return `400` with a problem detail.
+
+## Redirect behaviour (FR-2)
+
+A link with `maxClicks = N` redirects (`302`) exactly N times. Every later request returns `410 Gone`, the same
+status used for expired and deactivated links. Refused redirects are not counted.
+
+The limit is enforced by a single conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE click_count < max_clicks`, so concurrent
+redirects can never exceed it.
+
+## Metadata (FR-3)
+
+`GET /api/v1/links/{code}` returns `maxClicks` and `remainingClicks` (both `null` for unlimited links).
+
+## Compatibility (FR-4)
+
+- Migration `V2__add_link_max_clicks.sql` adds a nullable column; existing rows mean "unlimited".
+- Links without `maxClicks` follow the exact previous code path.
+- An `Idempotency-Key` replay must repeat the same `maxClicks`; a different value returns `409`.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- The limit cannot be changed after creation.
+- Response objects gained two fields; clients with strict deserialisation must accept unknown properties.
